^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
KNOWNBUG: this exposes a genuine std::span constructor bug, and the test was
previously an UNSOUND vacuous pass.

Before on-demand iterator member-function instantiation, instantiating
std::span<int> failed to complete its iterator type (the C++20 member alias
`iterator_concept = __iter_concept<_Iterator>` threw while the iterator was
incomplete, abandoning the iterator's class body and dropping all its member
functions).  That propagated into the elaboration of `std::span<int> s(arr, 3)`,
truncating main right after the `int arr[]` declaration: the span object, the
constructor call and BOTH __CPROVER_assert calls were dropped, so the old CORE
test (which only matched `^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$`) passed vacuously --
`--show-properties` listed neither `size` nor `first`.

With the iterator's `iterator_concept` alias kept lazy ([iterator.concepts.general]
+ [temp.inst]/1-2), the iterator's member functions are instantiated, main is no
longer truncated, and both assertions are now genuinely generated.

Constructor *selection* is now also fixed: the constrained span(_It, _End)
iterator-sentinel constructor's requires-clause `!is_convertible_v<_End,
size_type>` is now enforced (its substituted atomic constraint is constant-
folded before the tri-state evaluation), so span(arr, N) correctly selects
span(pointer, size_type) and `size` holds.

`first` still FAILS: span(pointer, size_type)'s body sets
`_M_ptr = std::to_address(__first)`, but std::to_address<T>(T*)'s (system-header)
body is itself discarded on a thrown exception during conversion, so _M_ptr is
left nondeterministic and the element read fails -- a further, separate
front-end gap ([temp.inst]/2-4 on-demand instantiation), now exposed.

Reclassify CORE once std::to_address's body converts so _M_ptr is the supplied
pointer and the populated span verifies genuinely.
